From vector produce

\vec{A} \times(\vec{B} \times \vec{C})=(\vec{B} \cdot \vec{C}) \vec{B}-(\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B}) \vec{C}

Given that $\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B}=0, \vec{A} \cdot \vec{C}=\mathrm{C}$ then $\vec{A} \times(\vec{B} \times \vec{C})=0$ thus vector $A$ is paralles to $\vec{B} \times \vec{C}$

Net force along $x$-axis zero.
Let the unknown force be $F$ along $x$-axis

\begin{aligned}
& \therefore F+1 \sin 30^{\circ}+2 \sin 30^{\circ}-4 \sin 30^{\circ}=0 \\
& \therefore|F|=0.5 \mathrm{~N}
\end{aligned}

$\vec{a}=2 \hat{i}+3 \hat{i}+8 \hat{k}, b=4 \hat{j}+3 \hat{i}+\alpha \hat{k}\begin{aligned}
& \vec{a} \cdot \vec{b}=0, \text { if } a^{-1} \cdot b^{-1} \\
& \therefore(2 \hat{i}+3 \hat{j}+8 \hat{k}) \cdot(-4 i+4 j+\alpha k)=\frac{1}{2} \\
& \Rightarrow \alpha=-\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}$

The heat produced in a wire due to current flow is given by

\begin{aligned}
H & =I^2 R t \\
\therefore \frac{\Delta H}{H} & =\frac{2 \Delta I}{I}+\frac{\Delta R}{R}+\frac{\Delta t}{t} \\
& =2 \times 0.02+0.01+0.01=0.06=6 \%
\end{aligned}
